<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos Montúfar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Carlos de Montúfar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos Montúfar was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> aristocrat and soldier, son of the Marquis of Selva Alegre. Sent to Quito in 1810 as a royal commissioner of the Regency, he sided with the patriots and promoted the second junta. He commanded its forces against the royalists, later fought for independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> and was executed by the Spanish in 1816.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-montufar.jpg" alt="Carlos Montúfar" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Salas Alzamora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_de_Mont%C3%BAfar_y_Larrea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Montúfar was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> on 2 November 1780, the third son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> nobles Juan Pío de Montúfar y Larrea, II Marquis of Selva Alegre, and Teresa de Larrea y Villavicencio. He graduated in 1800 from St. Thomas Aquinas University, where he studied philosophy and the humanities.</p><p>In 1802 he joined the German scientist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> and the French botanist Aimé Bonpland, who had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> that January. The choice caused jealousy: the Colombian inventor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jose-de-caldas">Francisco José de Caldas</a>, who had been turned down, complained of it. Montúfar climbed volcanoes of the Andes with them, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimborazo">Chimborazo</a> in June 1802, and travelled on through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he met political figures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a> in 1804 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> invited Humboldt there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He went with Humboldt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1804, living with him among a group of young South Americans that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>. In 1805 he began military training at the Royal Academy of Nobles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> he served as aide-de-camp to General Francisco Javier Castaños at the Battle of Bailén in 1808, and he was then named royal commissioner for the Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, with broad autonomy from the viceroys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><p>Learning that his father had taken part in the Quito Revolution, he returned in 1810 and helped form the short-lived State of Quito, which adopted its own constitution under Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-cuero-y-caicedo">José de Cuero y Caicedo</a>. He led its defence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toribio-montes">Toribio Montes</a>, Melchor Aymerich and Juan de Sámano. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> on 1 December 1812 he hid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> in Cayambe and the Los Chillos Valley, was arrested, tried for treason and sent towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, but escaped at Panama and joined the Liberation Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, where he rose to colonel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Pasto Campaign under General Manuel Roergas Serviez ended in defeat, Montúfar fought at the Battle of Cuchilla del Tambo on 29 June 1816. Captured and sentenced to death by Juan de Sámano, he was shot in the back as a traitor at Buga on 31 July 1816. Humboldt later wrote of him with sorrow. For the centenary of the Battle of Pichincha in 1922 his remains were taken to Quito Metropolitan Cathedral, and the moth Hellinsia montufari bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carlos de Montúfar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A second junta formed in September 1810, promoted by <b>Carlos Montúfar</b>, a royal commissioner who sided with the patriots; Bishop José Cuero y Caicedo later became its president.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-quito">State of Quito</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Died in Buga, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-0.22,-78.51&amp;year=1803" aria-label="Quito on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2369 2172 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carlos Montúfar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2819 2442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2819 2442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-0.22,-78.51&amp;year=1803">What was here in 1803? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
